virglrenderer: the last part of the guest GPU memory budget is kept for the VM's desktop

What: the budget (virgl-resource-memory-budget.patch, OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_MB, default
three quarters of the Mac's memory) gets a desktop reserve at its top. Apps and Venus
memory stop below it; the desktop's own GPU contexts (OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_DESKTOP,
default "Hyprland,quickshell,hyprlock": the guest's kernel names a context after its
process; hyprlock is the lock screen, which may start with the apps' share full)
may use the whole budget. Screens and cursors keep their 256 MB past the budget.
The reserve is a sixteenth of the Mac's memory, at least 512 MB and at most 2 GB, never
more than a quarter of the budget (8 GB Mac: 512 MB of 6 GB; 16 GB: 1 GB of 12 GB;
64 GB: 2 GB of 48 GB); OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_RESERVE_MB sets another, 0 turns it off.
- The guest's kernel makes a resource before it says for which context: it attaches
  it to that context right after (what virgl-resource-budget-context-loss.patch relies
  on). A resource that does not fit the apps' share but fits the whole budget is made
  "for the desktop only" and its handle remembered (the last 64). The first GL context
  that attaches it decides: the desktop's keeps it; an app's context is lost at once
  and told (GL_GUILTY_CONTEXT_RESET with mesa-virgl-reset-status.patch), with one log
  line that says why, and the resource gives its memory back at once (its pipe
  resource is dropped, virgl_resource_drop_pipe; the handle stays until the guest
  frees it, like a refused one). A lost app whose driver was not told (stock guest
  Mesa) goes on making resources: those past the share are dropped the same way, so it
  cannot use up the desktop's part (seen in a VM: a lost Chromium made 5K buffers in a
  loop until the whole budget was full and Hyprland's next one was refused). A
  context lost this way is switched away at once, so its next submit is refused. So
  the app that fills the memory loses its context, not Hyprland (a black VM) or the
  bar. A pipe resource (made in a context's own commands, so its context is known) is
  judged at once: an app's past its share is refused.
- macOS's memory pressure (virgl-darwin-memory-pressure.patch) works the same way: a
  big resource macOS has no room for (critical, or warn and bigger than what is left)
  is made for the desktop only instead of refused, after the same trim and looks. The
  desktop's own is never held back for pressure, only by the budget.
- The status file (OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_STATUS) also has apps_mb and reserve_mb, and why
  each lost context was lost: lost_why (the last) and lost_recent_why (with
  lost_recent): guard (the budget or the apps' share), pressure (macOS short of
  memory) or error (anything else). OmacVM.app says so when the desktop goes black.
- The rules are in a new header, src/virgl_gpu_guard.h, with no state: the shared
  counter's charge loop asks it for each try, so Venus threads and the renderer agree.
Why: on a MacBook Air M2 with 8 GB (2026-10-06, tracks/air-power-stress.md) a browser
with big WebGL pages filled the 6 GB budget while macOS still said "normal"; the
next refusal hit Hyprland's next buffer: its context was lost and the VM went black
(and OmacVM.app restarts the desktop, closing every app). The budget is there to stop
a runaway VM, and on a small Mac a browser gets there. Whoever filled the memory should
lose, not the desktop.
Threading: the handles live in virglrenderer.c (QEMU's main loop, like the refused
handles); the counter is atomic (Venus charges from its ring threads).
Upstream: not sent (depends on the budget, which upstream does not have).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-gpu-guard-policy.sh (CI and the runtime build: the header's
rules: reserve per Mac size, limits, verdicts with and without pressure, names);
Tests/virgl/test-resource-budget.c (build time, Apple's software renderer): mode
reserve (64 MB budget, 16 MB reserve: chromium's resource past 48 MB loses chromium and
gives its memory back, what the lost chromium makes next is dropped, Hyprland and
quickshell keep theirs up to 64 MB, nothing past it; pipe resources by a
known context; a desktop-only handle freed before anyone takes it; the status file's
apps_mb, reserve_mb and why), modes critical, warn and levelfile (a big resource is
for the desktop only: an app that takes it is lost, Hyprland keeps it), mode default
(the reserve for this Mac's memory). In a VM (MacBook Pro, 3.0.3 runtime with this
virglrenderer, OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_MB=3072, Chromium windows of WebGL pages with 512 MB
of textures each): e2e-mbp.sh in tracks/gpu-guard-8gb.md.
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/virgl_gpu_guard.h
@@ -0,0 +1,139 @@
+/*
+ * OmacVM: who may use the last part of the guest GPU memory budget
+ * (virgl-gpu-guard-desktop-reserve.patch). Rules only, no state:
+ * vrend_renderer.c follows them, Tests/virgl/test-gpu-guard-policy.c checks them.
+ *
+ * The budget (OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_MB, default three quarters of the Mac's memory)
+ * stops a runaway VM. Its last part, the desktop reserve, is kept for the VM's own
+ * desktop: the compositor and the shell. Apps (a browser with big WebGL pages,
+ * Venus memory) stop below it, and while macOS is short of memory, so on an 8 GB
+ * Mac the app that fills the memory loses its GPU context, not Hyprland (a black
+ * VM). Screens and cursors keep their 256 MB past the whole budget.
+ */
+#ifndef VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_H
+#define VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_H
+
+#include <stdbool.h>
+#include <stddef.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+#include <string.h>
+
+#define VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_RESERVE_MIN     (512ull << 20)
+#define VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_RESERVE_MAX     (2048ull << 20)
+#define VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DISPLAY_RESERVE (256ull << 20)
+/* The desktop's GPU contexts by name: the guest's kernel names a context after the
+ * process that made it. The guest chooses that name, so an app could take it; the
+ * reserve stays inside the budget either way. */
+#define VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DESKTOP_DEFAULT "Hyprland,quickshell,hyprlock"
+
+enum virgl_gpu_guard_who {
+   /* an app's resource (its context is known), Venus memory: up to the budget less
+    * the desktop reserve, and nothing big while macOS is short of memory */
+   VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_APP,
+   /* the desktop's own (its context is known): the whole budget, pressure or not */
+   VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DESKTOP,
+   /* screens and cursors: 256 MB past the budget, pressure or not */
+   VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DISPLAY,
+   /* a resource the guest's kernel makes before it says for which context (it
+    * attaches it to that context right after): what an app may have now, what only
+    * the desktop may have once the context is known */
+   VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_UNKNOWN,
+};
+
+enum virgl_gpu_guard_verdict {
+   VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_FITS,
+   /* made, but only the desktop may keep it: an app's context that takes it is lost */
+   VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DESKTOP_ONLY,
+   VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_REFUSED,
+};
+
+/* The desktop reserve for a Mac with MAC bytes of memory and a budget of BUDGET bytes
+ * (0: no budget, no reserve): a sixteenth of the Mac's memory, at least 512 MB and at
+ * most 2 GB (8 GB Mac: 512 MB, 16 GB: 1 GB, 32 GB and more: 2 GB), never more than a
+ * quarter of the budget. */
+static inline uint64_t virgl_gpu_guard_reserve(uint64_t mac, uint64_t budget)
+{
+   uint64_t r = mac / 16;
+
+   if (!budget)
+      return 0;
+   if (r < VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_RESERVE_MIN)
+      r = VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_RESERVE_MIN;
+   if (r > VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_RESERVE_MAX)
+      r = VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_RESERVE_MAX;
+   return r > budget / 4 ? budget / 4 : r;
+}
+
+/* How far WHO may take the bytes in use; UINT64_MAX without a budget. A reserve of
+ * more than half the budget (only by OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_RESERVE_MB) counts as half. */
+static inline uint64_t virgl_gpu_guard_limit(uint64_t budget, uint64_t reserve,
+                                             enum virgl_gpu_guard_who who)
+{
+   if (!budget)
+      return UINT64_MAX;
+   if (reserve > budget / 2)
+      reserve = budget / 2;
+   switch (who) {
+   case VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_APP:
+   case VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_UNKNOWN:
+      return budget - reserve;
+   case VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DESKTOP:
+      return budget;
+   case VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DISPLAY:
+   default:
+      return budget > UINT64_MAX - VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DISPLAY_RESERVE ?
+             UINT64_MAX : budget + VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DISPLAY_RESERVE;
+   }
+}
+
+static inline bool virgl_gpu_guard_fits(uint64_t in_use, uint64_t size, uint64_t limit)
+{
+   return size <= limit && in_use <= limit - size;
+}
+
+/* A new resource of SIZE bytes with IN_USE bytes in use. MAC_HAS_ROOM: macOS's
+ * memory pressure lets it through (virgl-darwin-memory-pressure.patch; always true
+ * for small resources). */
+static inline enum virgl_gpu_guard_verdict
+virgl_gpu_guard_verdict(uint64_t in_use, uint64_t size, uint64_t budget, uint64_t reserve,
+                        enum virgl_gpu_guard_who who, bool mac_has_room)
+{
+   uint64_t mine = virgl_gpu_guard_limit(budget, reserve, who);
+   uint64_t whole = virgl_gpu_guard_limit(budget, reserve, VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DESKTOP);
+
+   switch (who) {
+   case VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DISPLAY:
+   case VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DESKTOP:
+      return virgl_gpu_guard_fits(in_use, size, mine) ? VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_FITS : VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_REFUSED;
+   case VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_APP:
+      return mac_has_room && virgl_gpu_guard_fits(in_use, size, mine) ?
+             VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_FITS : VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_REFUSED;
+   case VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_UNKNOWN:
+   default:
+      if (mac_has_room && virgl_gpu_guard_fits(in_use, size, mine))
+         return VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_FITS;
+      return virgl_gpu_guard_fits(in_use, size, whole) ?
+             VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_DESKTOP_ONLY : VIRGL_GPU_GUARD_REFUSED;
+   }
+}
+
+/* Is NAME one of the comma-separated names in LIST (exact, case and all)? */
+static inline bool virgl_gpu_guard_is_desktop(const char *name, const char *list)
+{
+   size_t n = name ? strlen(name) : 0;
+
+   if (!n || !list)
+      return false;
+   for (const char *p = list; *p;) {
+      const char *end = strchr(p, ',');
+      size_t len = end ? (size_t)(end - p) : strlen(p);
+      if (len == n && !strncmp(p, name, n))
+         return true;
+      if (!end)
+         break;
+      p = end + 1;
+   }
+   return false;
+}
+
